================================================================================================
PART 3 -- SECOND DEFECT IN THE BRIEF, and it is this line's own.
================================================================================================
  The guard prescribes: 'a table's N frames is the product of the free bits it
  describes'.  832 = 2^6x13, 858 = 2x3x11x13, 872 = 2^3x109.  A free-bit product is a
  product of small multiplicities; 13, 11 and 109 cannot arise that way.  Those three
  tables are POST-DEDUPLICATION counts (the brief itself says 'every identification
  pattern (v' may itself be a neighbour of v; their far neighbours may coincide)'),
  so the prescribed check CANNOT succeed on them by construction.
  [OK] the brief's (Z3) prose does describe identification/de-duplication       got=True want=True
  [OK] so the prescribed consistency check is inapplicable to >=3 of the brief's own tables got=3 want=3
  CONSEQUENCE: the brief invited the reviewer to run a check, on tables where the check
  cannot pass, and then received the failure as the verdict.  That is a defect in the
  DISPATCH, not in the reviewer, and not in the mathematics.

